<h2>The case in outline</h2>
<p>FIR No. 88/2019 at Police Station Kotla Mubarakpur recorded that a boy of about eight had been sexually assaulted twice, roughly eight days apart, by a man known in the neighbourhood, who threatened to kill the child&rsquo;s mother if he spoke. The matter surfaced only when the family noticed the child bleeding while passing stool; a confrontation followed, and the police were called. The Fast Track Special Court (POCSO) at Saket convicted the appellant under Section 6 of the POCSO Act and Sections 377 and 506 IPC, sentencing him in March 2024 to twenty-five years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment on the POCSO count with ₹7.5 lakh compensation to the child.</p>
<p>On appeal, the defence pressed the familiar arsenal: variations in the child&rsquo;s accounts, uncertainty about the place of occurrence, a site plan prepared at the uncle&rsquo;s instance, inconclusive medical evidence, unexamined independent witnesses, and a motive of false implication rooted in enmity with the child&rsquo;s uncle.</p>

<h2>How the Court tested the child&rsquo;s evidence</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Reliability, not corroboration</h3><p>Applying the settled line of Supreme Court authority on child witnesses, the Court held that a conviction can rest on a child&rsquo;s testimony if it is credible and free of material infirmity; corroboration is caution&rsquo;s counsel, not a legal precondition.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Peripheral v. material discrepancies</h3><p>Differences about the exact date, the precise step on the staircase and who in the family learnt first were held peripheral. The Court found no mutually destructive versions of the core allegation.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Medical evidence in context</h3><p>No injury was found on examination — but two weeks had passed and no swab was taken. The doctor did not rule out penetration, so the medical record did not contradict the child.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Missing witnesses, no adverse inference</h3><p>The relatives not examined were not eyewitnesses. With the child himself cross-examined at length, their absence did not dent the prosecution.</p></div>
</div>
<p>School records proved the boy was under twelve, attracting Section 5(m); with the foundational facts established, the Section 29 presumption operated, and the enmity theory was too slender to rebut it. The precedents relied on by the defence were distinguished as cases of genuinely contradictory testimony without corroboration.</p>

<h2>The sentencing turn: which Section 6?</h2>
<p>The decisive sentencing question was temporal. Parliament amended Section 6 with effect from 16 August 2019, raising the minimum sentence from ten to twenty years. These assaults preceded the amendment. Following the Supreme Court&rsquo;s approach in <em>State of U.P. v. Sonu Kushwaha</em>, the Court applied the unamended provision:</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Unamended Section 6 (pre-16.08.2019)</h3><p>Rigorous imprisonment of not less than ten years, extendable to imprisonment for life, with fine.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>Amended Section 6 (from 16.08.2019)</h3><p>Rigorous imprisonment of not less than twenty years, extendable to imprisonment for life meaning the remainder of natural life, with fine — and death in specified cases.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Within the unamended range, the Court weighed the appellant&rsquo;s age of about twenty-two at the time, his clean record and long incarceration against the victim&rsquo;s tender age and the threats employed, concluding that ten years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment &ldquo;would adequately meet the ends of justice&rdquo;. The fine, the concurrent eighteen-month sentence under Section 506 IPC, and the ₹7.5 lakh compensation all stood; no separate sentence followed under Section 377 IPC by operation of Section 42 POCSO, and set-off for custody already undergone was allowed.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Conviction and sentence are separate battles.</strong> An appeal that cannot shake the child&rsquo;s testimony may still succeed substantially on the applicable sentencing regime.</li>
  <li><strong>Date of offence controls the statute.</strong> In POCSO prosecutions spanning the 2019 amendment, counsel on both sides must fix the date of occurrence with precision — decades of liberty can turn on it.</li>
  <li><strong>Defence theories need evidence.</strong> Enmity and false implication remain arguable, but against the Section 29 presumption they require proof, not suggestion.</li>
  <li><strong>Victim compensation survives sentence modification.</strong> Reduction of the prison term left the ₹7.5 lakh award untouched — the two operate on different axes.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Where the charge-sheet alleges incidents &ldquo;about a month before&rdquo; a 2019 FIR, the amendment date of 16 August 2019 becomes a live issue at framing of charge, not an afterthought at sentencing. Trial courts sentencing under the amended Section 6 for pre-amendment acts commit an error that appellate courts will correct — but only if the record fixes the timeline clearly.</p>
</div>
